    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d4e82e86_b560309c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-27T01:06:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I don\u0027t think we want to do this, it adds a lot of complexity and potential for bugs.\n\nThat\u0027s why all codes are guarded by a flag.\n\n\u003e For example, if you replace one binary with another, and the new one requires more/different shared libraries, those dependencies won\u0027t be encoded in the build system anymore.\n\nWe don\u0027t support such cases. Downloaded artifacts should match with the sources. For example: targets having the same build ID.\n\n\u003e Also, the build should not download anything from the network. If I understand this correctly you\u0027re not actually downloading stuff, but merely copying it out of another folder, but the naming is concerning.\n\nAs Justin said, \"download\" is a general term. In our first experiment, that will be pulling stuff from chained build, not downloading via network. PTAL https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1feYdtGQcsoF3VsPtm8Mz6jv2fh7pcupF6eyp5Kf7hqM/edit?usp\u003dsharing\u0026resourcekey\u003d0-zagSsrZY77ESIUEV_iB3EQ",
      "parentUuid": "93b104ed_2ca3a98e",
      "revId": "e8b71da5acc241258dcb4b50092b59ccfb14b6c7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "648ebac2_6d0e8c24",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074628
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-28T08:08:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This is for a limited use cases in CI build for saving the build resources.\n\nWe have so many better ways of doing that already, and this is explicitly discarding one of our best methods (incremental builds).\n\nDo you have performance results yet showing significant improvements over the other ways to do this? (using prebuilt images, etc)\n\n\u003e\u003e\u003e If I understand this correctly you\u0027re not actually downloading stuff, but merely copying it out of another folder, but the naming is concerning.\n\u003e\u003e Also, we want to provide replaceable download script for partners to use the feature in their own CI.\n\u003e As Justin said, \"download\" is a general term.\n\n+1 to the naming being misleading. Download is not a particularly general term: \"copy (data) from one computer system to another, typically over the internet\". And even if we allow partners to replace this script, it wouldn\u0027t be able to do any network communication.",
      "parentUuid": "d4e82e86_b560309c",
      "revId": "e8b71da5acc241258dcb4b50092b59ccfb14b6c7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e1d717b_b7623c30",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-28T14:43:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e We have so many better ways of doing that already, and this is explicitly discarding one of our best methods (incremental builds).\n\nThe target building the target files zip still uses incremental builds. Artifacts not pulled from the target files still can be done with incremental builds.\n\n\u003e Do you have performance results yet showing significant improvements over the other ways to do this? (using prebuilt images, etc)\n\nUsing prebuilt images is much more simpler, but that\u0027s not our final goal. For example, we may have a single target generating a superset of system_ext images of various targets. And then each target simply assembles needed artifacts. (e.g. target X builds foo.apk and bar.apk, target A pulls system_ext/app/foo/foo.apk, target B pulls system_ext/app/bar/bar.apk, etc.) There is no result yet, but we **believe** that most parts of system and system_ext (and possibly vendor for targets sharing the same chip) can be shared.",
      "parentUuid": "648ebac2_6d0e8c24",
      "revId": "e8b71da5acc241258dcb4b50092b59ccfb14b6c7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2391bc3b_eb31d855",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1146974
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-29T05:51:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Do you have performance results yet showing significant improvements over the other ways to do this? (using prebuilt images, etc)\n\nThis change is protected by a BUILD_WITH_PARTIAL_ARTIAFCT flag for experiment. This is not a final product, but we want to monitor some build metrics with the cf targets and improve the performance in more treble ways. That are, more SSI sharing between targets, more unbundled vendor build, and extending the solution for smaller source trees for building each image in the long term.\nThis is not only for CI build, but also for partners to have efficient build environment.",
      "parentUuid": "3e1d717b_b7623c30",
      "revId": "e8b71da5acc241258dcb4b50092b59ccfb14b6c7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
